## 1. Architecture Overview

```mermaid
graph TD
    A["Upload CSV/XLSX/JSON/Parquet"] --> B["FastAPI POST /api/cleaning/run"]
    B --> C["DuckDBEngine.load_from_bytes()"]
    C --> D["SchemaProfiler.profile_table()"]
    D --> E["ContextSlicer → slice_context(state, 'cleaning')"]
    E --> F["compress_column_meta_for_prompt()"]
    F --> G["LLMRouter.route(CLEANING_STRATEGY)"]
    G --> H{"LLM returned valid JSON?"}
    H -->|Yes| I["Parse CleaningOperation list"]
    H -->|No / 0 ops| J["generate_heuristic_cleaning_ops()"]
    I --> K["Polars Deterministic Rule Engine"]
    J --> K
    K --> L["df = lf.collect()"]
    L --> M["Write cleaned.parquet"]
    M --> N["Calculate data_quality_score"]
    N --> O["SSE Stream → Frontend UI"]
    O --> P["Cleaned Data Preview Table"]
```

### Core Design Principle
> **"Cleaning code generation LLM se nhi krna hai — Polars should clean, not Ollama."**

The cleaning agent uses a **2-phase architecture**:
- **Phase 1 (Strategy)**: LLM generates a JSON list of `CleaningOperation` objects describing *what* to do.
- **Phase 2 (Execution)**: A deterministic Polars rule engine maps each operation to a hardcoded `pl.Expr`, with **zero LLM code-generation calls**.

This eliminates the risk of LLM-generated code executing arbitrary operations while maintaining full Polars performance.

